New code (rival-pickup-block.test.ts:12-18):
```
function steerTo(car: CarView, p: PickupInstance): number {
  const want = Math.atan2(p.x - car.x, -(p.z - car.z));
  let err = want - car.heading;
  while (err > Math.PI) err -= 2 * Math.PI;
  while (err < -Math.PI) err += 2 * Math.PI;
  return Math.max(-1, Math.min(1, err * 2.5));
}
```
Existing helper (tests/sim/helpers.ts:163-168):
```
export function driveToward(world: World, x: number, z: number, carIndex = 0): CarInput {
  ...
  const desired = headingOf(x - car.x, z - car.z);   // = Math.atan2(dx, -dz)
  return { steer: clamp(wrapAngle(desired - car.heading) * 2.5, -1, 1), throttle: true, brake: false };
}
```
The heading formula, wrap, gain and clamp are the same. The only differences are that driveToward reads `world.cars[0]` instead of a CarView (same x, z and heading) and wraps to (−π, π] instead of [−π, π]. That makes no difference to the steer value except at exactly ±π, where both clamp to ±1 anyway. The `drive` callback already gets `world`, so it can call driveToward directly.
